WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.959
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.959 --> 00:00:15.610
Today we are going to do this and this and
this on a budget.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:21.100
Today, through the power of computer graphics
research works, we can simulate all these

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.330
amazing elastic interactions.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:29.920
If we are very patient that is, because they
take forever to compute.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:36.930
But, if we wish to run these simulations quicker,
what we can is increasing something that we

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:39.269
call the time step size.

00:00:39.269 --> 00:00:45.780
Usually, this means that the simulation takes
less time, but is also less accurate.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:50.789
Let’s see this phenomenon through a previous
method from just a year ago.

00:00:50.789 --> 00:00:57.239
Here, we set the time step size relatively
small, and drop an elastic barbarian ship

00:00:57.239 --> 00:00:58.929
onto these rods.

00:00:58.929 --> 00:01:06.000
And this is a challenging scene because the
ship is made out of half a million tiny elements,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.410
and we have to simulate their interactions
with the scene.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:11.990
How does this perform?

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:12.990
Uh-oh.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:13.990
This isn’t good.

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.050
Did you see the issues?

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:19.940
Issue number one is that the simulation is
unstable.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.280
Look.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.280
Things remain in motion when they shouldn’t.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:30.230
And two, this is also troubling.

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:31.700
Penetrations.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.450
Now, let’s increase the time step size.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.030
What do we expect to happen?

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:44.930
Well, now, we advance the simulation in bigger
chunks, so we should expect to miss even more

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:49.410
interactions in between these bigger steps.

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:51.180
And…whoa!

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.230
Sure enough.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:55.040
Even more instability, even more penetration.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.740
So, what is the solution?

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:03.180
Well, let’s have a look at this new method
and see if it can deal with this difficult

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:04.180
scene.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:10.879
Now, hold on to your papers, and…wow, I
am loving this.

00:02:10.879 --> 00:02:18.909
Issue number one, things coming to rest is
solved, and, issue number two, no penetrations.

00:02:18.909 --> 00:02:19.940
That is amazing.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:23.099
Now, what is so interesting here?

00:02:23.099 --> 00:02:28.920
Well, what you see here should not be possible
at all, because this new technique computes

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.049
a reduced simulation instead.

00:02:31.049 --> 00:02:34.160
This is a simulation on a budget.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:39.790
And not only that, but let’s increase the
time step size a little.

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:44.640
This means that we can advance the time when
in bigger chunks when computing the simulation,

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:51.849
at the cost of potentially missing important
interactions between these steps.

00:02:51.849 --> 00:02:59.389
Expect a bad simulation now, like with the
previous one, and…wow, this is amazing.

00:02:59.389 --> 00:03:01.310
It still looks fine.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:07.269
But we don’t know that for sure, because
we haven’t seen the reference simulation

00:03:07.269 --> 00:03:08.269
yet.

00:03:08.269 --> 00:03:10.080
So, you know what’s coming.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:11.080
Oh yes!

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:16.049
Let’s compare it to the reference simulation
that takes forever to compute.

00:03:16.049 --> 00:03:17.969
This looks great.

00:03:17.969 --> 00:03:22.950
And, let’s see… this looks great too.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:28.599
They don’t look the same, but if I were
asked which one reference is, and which is

00:03:28.599 --> 00:03:33.889
the cheaper, reduced simulation, I am not
sure if I would be able to tell.

00:03:33.889 --> 00:03:36.370
Are you able to tell?

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:41.569
Well, be careful with your answer, because
I have swapped the two.

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:47.269
In reality, this is the reference, and this
is the reduced simulation.

00:03:47.269 --> 00:03:49.799
Were you able to tell?

00:03:49.799 --> 00:03:51.919
Let me know in the comments below.

00:03:51.919 --> 00:03:54.269
And that is exactly the point!

00:03:54.269 --> 00:03:59.980
All this means that we got away with only
computing the simulation in bigger steps.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:01.909
So, why is that good?

00:04:01.909 --> 00:04:05.700
Well, of course, we get through it quicker!

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:09.809
Okay, but how much quicker?

00:04:09.809 --> 00:04:12.909
110 times quicker.

00:04:12.909 --> 00:04:14.400
What?

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:20.470
The two are close to equivalent, but this
is more than a 100 times quicker?

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.750
Sign me up, right away!

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:28.570
Note that this is still not real time, but
we are firmly in the seconds per frame domain,

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:33.840
so we don’t need an all nighter for such
a simulation, just a coffee break.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.740
Now note that this particular scene is really
suited for the new technique, other scenes

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:44.210
aren’t typically a hundred times faster,
but, worst case scenario is when we throw

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:52.410
around a bunch of furballs, but even that
is at least ten to fifteen times faster.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:53.599
What does that mean?

00:04:53.599 --> 00:05:00.379
Well, an all-nighter simulation can be done
maybe not during a coffee break, but during

00:05:00.379 --> 00:05:02.000
a quick little nap.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:08.759
Yes, we can rest like this tiny dinosaur for
a while, and by the time we wake up, the simulation

00:05:08.759 --> 00:05:14.180
is done, and we can count on it being close
to the real deal.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.409
So good!

00:05:15.409 --> 00:05:21.640
Just make sure to keep the friction high while
resting here, or otherwise…this happens.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:31.360
So, from now on, we get better simulations,
up to a hundred times faster.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:53.129
What 

00:05:53.129 --> 00:06:03.139
a time to be alive!

00:06:03.139 --> 00:06:11.740
Thanks 
for watching and for your generous support,

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:13.440
and I'll see you next time!

